Ladybird positions the thumb along the inline axis of the range
control. Its endpoints are inset from the track ends by half the
thumb size. Blink’s SliderThumbElement::SetPositionFromPoint,
WebKit’s SliderThumbElement::setPositionFromPoint, and Gecko’s
nsRangeFrame::GetValueAtEventPoint also use the available travel to
map the pointer to a value.

Chrome 158.0.8127.38, Firefox 151.0, and WebKit 26.5 agreed on both
endpoints for clicks and drags in all five writing modes and both
directions. An author-sized 12px thumb traveled 108px along a 120px
vertical track in each engine. Rotating the input or its parent by
90 degrees counterclockwise put the maximum at the top in all three.

At 70% on a 160px horizontal track, the 16px thumb starts 100.8px
along the track. Ladybird lays the thumb out there and snaps its box
during painting. Chrome, Firefox, and WebKit also painted the thumb
with the same edge coverage as a circle laid out at that offset.

When the thumb has no layout box, Ladybird measures pointer travel
across the entire track. Firefox 151.0 also accepted clicks and
drags on the track. Chrome 158.0.8127.38 and WebKit 26.5 left the
value unchanged. Keeping the track interactive preserves the behavior
of pages that hide the thumb and draw their own indicator.
